Let the vertices of the tetrahedron be $A = (0,1,2)$, $B = (3,0,1)$, $C = (4,3,6)$, and $D = (2,3,2)$. The volume $V$ of a tetrahedron with vertices $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$ is given by
\[ V = \frac{1}{6} |\overrightarrow{AB} \cdot (\overrightarrow{AC} \times \overrightarrow{AD})|. \]
We have
\begin{align*} \overrightarrow{AB} &= \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix} \\ \overrightarrow{AC} &= \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 3 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 2 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} \\ \overrightarrow{AD} &= \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} \end{align*}
Then
\begin{align*} \overrightarrow{AC} \times \overrightarrow{AD} &= \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 2 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} \times \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} (2)(0) - (4)(2) \\ (4)(2) - (4)(0) \\ (4)(2) - (2)(2) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -8 \\ 8 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} \end{align*}
Then
\begin{align*} \overrightarrow{AB} \cdot (\overrightarrow{AC} \times \overrightarrow{AD}) &= \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} -8 \\ 8 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} = (3)(-8) + (-1)(8) + (-1)(4) = -24 - 8 - 4 = -36 \end{align*}

